Mainline Linux for the NVIDIA SHIELD Tablet (TN8)
=================================================

This kernel is being tested exclusively on an NVIDIA SHIELD Tablet K1 with the
NVIDIA SHIELD Android 5.0/L bootloader. It *should* work on other models, but
not necessarily other bootloader versions. Use this kernel at your own risk,
as it is in a fairly experimental state.

Android M/N bootloader compatibility is not planned short term due to seemingly
stricter requirements (no initramfs, smaller kernel image size).

In other words, when using this kernel, you agree to the possibility of hardware
damage. I've had none so far, but cannot guarantee you won't.

This port uses `tegra_defconfig` to store its default kernel configuration.

Note: Compile with `-march=armv7ve -mtune=cortex-a15`.

Current Status
--------------

Fully functional:

* All CPU cores
* Internal eMMC
* Hardware keys (volume up, volume down, power)
* Fuel gauge (using custom driver `lc709203f`)
* Charger (using `bq24193`)
* Power monitor (using `ina3221`)
* Memory controller
* Thermal sensors (using `tegra_soctherm` and `generic_adc_thermal`)

Fully functional, with quirks:

* Audio (RT5639 codec)
* Bluetooth (using `hci_bcm`)
* Wi-Fi (using `brcmfmac`)

Partially functional:

* Display (using `tegradrm`): Instability found under high system load.
* GPU (using `nouveau`): Pretty unstable, works only with a patch to Mesa :
  https://github.com/JordanViknar/mesa-tegra/tree/tegra-fixes
  If you want to use the framebuffer, disable Nouveau in the kernel config.

Configured, untested :

* Accelerometer + gyroscope (using `inv-mpu6050-i2c`)
* Magnetometer (using `ak8975`)
* Thermal zones

Non-functional / not worked on yet:

* SD card reader
* USB device and host
* Touchscreen: No driver in mainline.
* GPS
* Camera

Unplanned (excluding external contributions) :

* 3G/LTE: My SHIELD Tablet is a K1, it only has Wi-Fi.
* Stylus: I do not have the stylus, so I cannot test it.

Elements not listed above are either not tested/worked on yet, or not
applicable to this device.

Custom Drivers
--------------

Drivers that are unique to this kernel port and not mainlined:

* Fuel gauge (lc709203f): Custom driver for the fuel gauge chip. Ported from
  Zephyr RTOS. Needs more testing, but seems to work fine.
  See `drivers/power/supply/lc709203f-fuel-gauge.c`

Quirks
------

Minor:

* Audio: Userspace needs custom ALSA UCM and Wireplumber configuration to work
  properly with the tablet's audio hardware. You can find it at :
  https://github.com/JordanViknar/linux-tn8-rootfs
* Wi-Fi & Bluetooth: Requires firmware files inside initramfs. Requires booting
  downstream kernel first, then directly restarting into mainline.
* GPU: Requires firmware files inside initramfs.

Major:

* Bootloader: Baking too many modules into the kernel itself will cause kernel
  module glitches and initramfs failures on boot. Reason unknown.
* Display: Do not reboot from mainline into downstream kernel. It glitches the
  display and may cause hardware damage if left in this state too long.
  Rebooting from downstream into mainline is unaffected.

Implementation Notes
--------------------

* Audio: The SHIELD Tablet does not wire up a dedicated headphone-detect GPIO.
  Instead, the RT5639 uses its built-in jack detection (`realtek,jack-detect-source`),
  requiring a small generic addition to `tegra_asoc_machine.c` to support codecs
  with `.set_jack()` but no HP detect GPIO.
  Audio routing (speaker/headphones, microphone selection, etc.) is supposed to
  be handled entirely in userspace via ALSA UCM + WirePlumber.
  Because the RT5639 exposes speaker and headphone outputs through a shared PCM,
  UCM presents them as separate profiles rather than switchable ports.
  WirePlumber's `auto-profile` rule provides automatic switching.
  This seems to be an upstream rt5640 UCM limitation also affecting other devices.
* Memory controller: Couldn't get some of the downstream kernel's values to
  work on mainline. Using the values from Aaron Kling's `tegra124-ardbeg-emc.dtsi`
  seems to be stable.
  See `arch/arm/boot/dts/nvidia/tegra124-tn8-emc.dtsi`.
* SMP: On the Android L bootloader (may not be exclusive to it), PSCI CPU_ON
  claims success but never actually powers on CPU1-3. Worked around by using
  a PSCI CPU_ON call on CPU0 (already running) purely to register the reset
  vector with firmware, then powering CPU1-3 on directly via PMC instead of
  PSCI, matching what the Tegra4Linux kernel does.
  See `arch/arm/mach-tegra/reset.c` and `arch/arm/mach-tegra/platsmp.c`.

Credits
-----------

Previous mainline ports:

* Alexandre Courbot, for his original basic Linux 3.17 mainline port, conveniently 
  saved from deletion by Andrew Querol:
  https://github.com/winsock/linux/tree/st8
* Aaron Kling, for his more recent partial DTS for the NVIDIA SHIELD Tablet:
  https://gitlab.incom.co/CM-Shield/android_hardware_nvidia_t124_lineage/-/blob/lineage-24.0/tegra124-tn8.dts

Other mainline ports:

* tegra124-xiaomi-mocha.dts: Similar device used for some comparisons and
  general structure.
* tegra114-roth.dts & tegra114-tn7.dts: NVIDIA devices with similar bootloaders.

Downstream kernel:

* The original Linux4Tegra port's downstream DTS was used as base :
  https://xdaforums.com/t/linux4tegra-r23-1-r24-1-beta-for-the-shield-tablet.2985238/
  https://github.com/bogsen/STLinux-Kernel
  https://gist.github.com/JordanViknar/8fef373312b60890d718846927095e6b
* LineageOS's downstream kernel for the NVIDIA SHIELD Tablet was also used as base :
  https://github.com/LineageOS/android_kernel_nvidia_shield

-----------------------------------------------

Linux kernel
============

The Linux kernel is the core of any Linux operating system. It manages hardware,
system resources, and provides the fundamental services for all other software.

Quick Start
-----------

* Report a bug: See Documentation/admin-guide/reporting-issues.rst
* Get the latest kernel: https://kernel.org
* Build the kernel: See Documentation/admin-guide/quickly-build-trimmed-linux.rst
* Join the community: https://lore.kernel.org/

Essential Documentation
-----------------------

All users should be familiar with:

* Building requirements: Documentation/process/changes.rst
* Code of Conduct: Documentation/process/code-of-conduct.rst
* License: See COPYING

Documentation can be built with make htmldocs or viewed online at:
https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/


Who Are You?
============

Find your role below:

* New Kernel Developer - Getting started with kernel development
* Academic Researcher - Studying kernel internals and architecture
* Security Expert - Hardening and vulnerability analysis
* Backport/Maintenance Engineer - Maintaining stable kernels
* System Administrator - Configuring and troubleshooting
* Maintainer - Leading subsystems and reviewing patches
* Hardware Vendor - Writing drivers for new hardware
* Distribution Maintainer - Packaging kernels for distros
* AI Coding Assistant - LLMs and AI-powered development tools


